In this lesson we'll look at how to simplify algebraic expressions so they look neat and are easier to work with. There are really just two habits to keep in mind. The first is collecting like terms. Like terms are the ones that look the same underneath, meaning the same letter raised to the same power. So three x plus five x is just eight x, because the x parts match up. And four x squared minus x squared gives you three x squared. But watch out, x and x y are not like terms, since one has a y attached and the other does not, so you leave them separate. The second habit is the reverse of the distributive law, sometimes called factoring out. If every term inside a group shares a common piece, you can pull that piece to the front and tidy things up. A quick checklist is to expand any brackets first, then collect like terms, and finally factor out the greatest common factor if you're asked to. Now let's see it in action.